+# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
 # Design analysis for the D4 variant
-# ----------------------------------
-# spec_variant:
-#   preserved_structure:
-#     grid: 4-13 rows x 10-15 cols, bg=0
-#     palette: 2xN or Nx2 on one outer edge; outer line gives border-key colors, inner line gives fill colors
-#     sprites: 2-4 hollow sprites, separated from each other and from the palette by >=1 bg cell
-#     output_rule: fill enclosed bg holes only; palette itself stays unchanged
-#   src_change:
-#     before: each sprite border was monochrome, so the hole-adjacent dominant color and the sprite color were identical
-#     after: each sprite border uses >=2 colors; the lookup key is the majority color over the entire sprite border
-#   concrete_visualization:
-#     - Each filled sprite uses two border colors:
-#       major = palette key whose mapped fill is the correct answer
-#       accent = another palette key that appears more often around the hole boundary than major
-#     - Cells on the outer-only part of the border stay major, so the full border still has a strict major-color majority
-#     - Result: a human can see a multicolor ring where the color most exposed to the hole is not the one chosen in output
-#   behavior_trace:
-#     original_logic_example:
-#       sprite border counts around hole: red=5, blue=3; full border: red=9, blue=3 -> original solver picks red
-#     variant_logic_example:
-#       hole-adjacent counts: blue=5, red=3; full border: red=7, blue=5 -> old local rule would pick blue, new rule must pick red
-#     difference:
-#       the decision site moves from "local adjacency histogram of the hole" to "global histogram of the whole shell touching the hole"
-#   complexity_calibration:
-#     - Only two border colors are used per sprite, so the change is visible but not noisy
-#     - Accent color is also a palette key for filled sprites, which prevents degeneration back to the original task
-#     - Fill values remain distinct, so choosing the wrong key leads to a visibly wrong fill
-#   implicit_assumption_audit:
-#     - _best_palette / _palette_mapping only assume palette keys are unique on the palette edge; this still holds
-#     - The old _fill_enclosed_bg_regions assumed a hole's correct key is the dominant adjacent color; this no longer holds and must be replaced
-#     - New main() assumes every hole is surrounded by one visual sprite, but that sprite may be split into several 4-connected pieces
-#       (for example diamond-like near-connected shells). Therefore the solver unions all non-bg components touching the hole before
-#       computing the majority color
-#   placement_legality:
-#     - Generator still uses padded occupancy, so sprites and palette never overlap or touch 4-neigh
-#     - Sprites are only accepted when their hole-facing cells and outer-only cells allow:
-#         accent majority on each hole boundary
-#         major strict majority on the entire shell
-#     - This guarantees the new solver's assumptions are true on generated samples
-#   observability_and_cleanliness:
-#     - The border remains a single obvious shell around each hole; only border colors vary
-#     - Output still only changes hole interiors, so the causal link between border colors and fill color remains legible
-#     - No hidden ordering by color id is used; the rule is driven by visible frequency on the sprite border
-#   color_inference:
-#     - Palette size stays small (2-5)
-#     - Each sample contains repeated evidence that "overall majority border color" is the palette lookup key
-#     - Because accent colors are valid palette keys too, the solver cannot succeed by ignoring the new majority mechanism
+#
+# 1) Concrete behavior:
+#    - The edge palette is still a 2xN / Nx2 legend. Its outer line lists lookup keys.
+#    - Hollow sprites are no longer monochrome. A single sprite now mixes 2 colors on
+#      the same connected border object.
+#    - The intended lookup key is the sprite-wide majority color, not the color that
+#      happens to dominate immediately around an individual hole cell.
+#    - Generator therefore builds protruding irregular sprites: the protrusions carry
+#      extra majority-color mass, while the ring around the hole may show more accent
+#      cells. This makes the new rule visually necessary.
+#
+# 2) Old vs new execution at the critical step:
+#    - Old solver: for one enclosed bg region, count adjacent border colors around that
+#      hole and use the most frequent adjacent mapped color.
+#    - New variant: first identify which connected multicolor sprite encloses the hole,
+#      then take the majority color over the whole sprite component and use that key.
+#    - Example: a ring around one hole has 5 accent cells and 3 key-color cells, but
+#      the same sprite also has 3 protrusion cells in the key color. Old logic sees
+#      the hole-adjacent 5>3 accent and would choose the wrong key. New logic sees the
+#      full sprite counts key=6, accent=5 and fills from the key color instead.
+#
+# 3) Hidden assumptions that break under the variant:
+#    - Original _fill_enclosed_bg_regions assumed "adjacent border color" equals the
+#      sprite identity. That only held because every sprite was monochrome.
+#    - The original code also ignored cross-hole consistency; with multicolor borders,
+#      different holes in the same sprite must still resolve through one shared key.
+#    - Palette parsing required unique palette keys, but not unique sprite colors. That
+#      remains valid, so the palette finder stays unchanged.
+#
+# 4) Placement legality:
+#    - Sprites are kept at least one cell away from each other, from the palette, and
+#      from the outer grid boundary. This prevents palette/sprite ambiguity once sprite
+#      colors may reuse palette-key colors.
+#    - Each generated sprite has one enclosed hole, stays connected, and has at least a
+#      few non-hole-adjacent protrusion cells so majority-vs-local-color can differ.
+#
+# 5) Visual observability / cleanliness:
+#    - Every sprite uses exactly 2 colors, keeping the pattern readable.
+#    - At least one filled sprite per sample is forced to have a different hole-local
+#      dominant color than its sprite-wide majority color, so the D4 behavior is not
+#      a cosmetic no-op.
+#    - Accent colors are usually other palette keys, giving the human solver a visible
+#      cue that frequency, not mere presence, determines the lookup.
