    22	# Design analysis for the D4 variant
    23	#
    24	# 1) Concrete behavior:
    25	#    - The edge palette is still a 2xN / Nx2 legend. Its outer line lists lookup keys.
    26	#    - Hollow sprites are no longer monochrome. A single sprite now mixes 2 colors on
    27	#      the same connected border object.
    28	#    - The intended lookup key is the sprite-wide majority color, not the color that
    29	#      happens to dominate immediately around an individual hole cell.
    30	#    - Generator therefore builds protruding irregular sprites: the protrusions carry
    31	#      extra majority-color mass, while the ring around the hole may show more accent
    32	#      cells. This makes the new rule visually necessary.
    33	#
    34	# 2) Old vs new execution at the critical step:
    35	#    - Old solver: for one enclosed bg region, count adjacent border colors around that
    36	#      hole and use the most frequent adjacent mapped color.
    37	#    - New variant: first identify which connected multicolor sprite encloses the hole,
    38	#      then take the majority color over the whole sprite component and use that key.
    39	#    - Example: a ring around one hole has 5 accent cells and 3 key-color cells, but
    40	#      the same sprite also has 3 protrusion cells in the key color. Old logic sees
    41	#      the hole-adjacent 5>3 accent and would choose the wrong key. New logic sees the
    42	#      full sprite counts key=6, accent=5 and fills from the key color instead.
    43	#
    44	# 3) Hidden assumptions that break under the variant:
    45	#    - Original _fill_enclosed_bg_regions assumed "adjacent border color" equals the
    46	#      sprite identity. That only held because every sprite was monochrome.
    47	#    - The original code also ignored cross-hole consistency; with multicolor borders,
    48	#      different holes in the same sprite must still resolve through one shared key.
    49	#    - Palette parsing required unique palette keys, but not unique sprite colors. That
    50	#      remains valid, so the palette finder stays unchanged.
    51	#
    52	# 4) Placement legality:
    53	#    - Sprites are kept at least one cell away from each other, from the palette, and
    54	#      from the outer grid boundary. This prevents palette/sprite ambiguity once sprite
    55	#      colors may reuse palette-key colors.
    56	#    - Each generated sprite has one enclosed hole, stays connected, and has at least a
    57	#      few non-hole-adjacent protrusion cells so majority-vs-local-color can differ.
    58	#
    59	# 5) Visual observability / cleanliness:
    60	#    - Every sprite uses exactly 2 colors, keeping the pattern readable.
    61	#    - At least one filled sprite per sample is forced to have a different hole-local
    62	#      dominant color than its sprite-wide majority color, so the D4 behavior is not
    63	#      a cosmetic no-op.
    64	#    - Accent colors are usually other palette keys, giving the human solver a visible
    65	#      cue that frequency, not mere presence, determines the lookup.
    66	# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
    67	
    68	
    69	def _bg_boundary(g: np.ndarray) -> int:
    70	    """Most common color along the 1-cell outer border."""
    71	    border = np.concatenate([g[0, :], g[-1, :], g[1:-1, 0], g[1:-1, -1]])
    72	    vals, cnts = np.unique(border, return_counts=True)
    73	    return int(vals[cnts.argmax()])
    74	
